# Beat for demo step 4: Eve's truncated log, no root type of its own.
#
# Reverse-crafts a schema for the log from the fields the client's corpus
# does know. Each step says what to do and lets the presenter do it; an
# `advance_when` predicate moves on once it is done. Each step re-declares
# the view the previous action left (folds, caret, heat cues), so the
# transition does not move anything on screen. The first override — the
# synthetic `Capture` type — is pre-filled; the others are made by hand
# (`t` to open the override pane, `o` to pre-fill the command from it).
#
# What it relies on, true of any log the demo produces: entry 1 is a
# request and entry 2 a response (the server logs both, in order); the cut
# entry at the end is the only one flagged TRUNCATED_BYTES; Eve smuggled
# `whoami` at some point. Which entry carries it varies, hence the text
# search ("whoami") rather than a path.
#
# Step text is written for a 120-column line (spec 0400).
#
# Invocation (from grehack2026.sh):
#   protolens --descriptor-set life.desc eve/server.log \
#       --script beats/logfile
#
# Re-pin (must print nothing):
#   protolens --descriptor-set life.desc eve/server.log \
#       --script beats/logfile script | grep 'error:'

title: A blob with no schema

steps:
  - text: |
      eve/server.log, opened against the client's life.desc. No known type fits the log as a whole: instead of a type
      name, protolens shows a generic `message`.

      👋 Press `1` to fold it, so its top-level messages fit on screen.
    node: /
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - not:
        - visible: /1/1

  - text: |
      Hundreds of top-level messages, all with field number 42 — one per message Eve's server saw.

      👋 Press `G` to go to the end, then `k` to step onto the last one.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - caret: TRUNCATED_BYTES

  - text: |
      The last one is not a message like the others: protolens could not decode it, and shows its raw bytes.

      👋 Press `Ctrl-E` to go to the end of the line: flagged TRUNCATED, the server was cut off mid-write.
         A standard decoder rejects the whole file for these last bytes; protolens reads up to them.
         Then press `Ctrl-A` to come back to its start, `gg` to go back to the top, and `2` to look one level deeper.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    search: '^ *42:.*TRUNCATED'
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - visible: /1/1

  - text: |
      Every field 42 has the same shape. What are they? The client's corpus may recognize what is inside.

      👋 Press `i` to turn on the heat cues: how well the corpus can type each message.
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - heat_cues: findings

  - text: |
      Hover over the heat cue at the end of the first message's field 1: a StepRequest. The second message's field 2:
      a StepResponse. A request, then its response — these are captures of the game's traffic, one per message.

      👋 Press space.
    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      Let's say so: name the field `capture`, and give it a type of its own, a synthetic `Capture`.

      👋 Press Enter to commit the prefilled override.
    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "42 [{]  #@ message"
    command: override /:42 --as-new Capture --cardinality repeated --field-name capture
    advance_when:
      - type: /1 Capture

  - text: |
      👍 Every field 42 is now a `capture`. Now pin what is inside: field 1 of the first capture is a StepRequest.

      👋 Double-click the heat cue, and choose StepRequest.
    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/1
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/1 grehack.life.v1.StepRequest

  - text: |
      👍 And the second capture, field 2: its answer, a StepResponse. Requests and responses, in turn.

      👋 Double-click the heat cue, and choose StepResponse.
    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /2/1
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /2/1 grehack.life.v1.StepResponse

  - text: |
      Field 3, a counter that climbs by one every two captures: the generation.

      👋 Press `t`, then `o`; in the prefilled override, rename the field: `--field-name generation`. Press Enter.
    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: /1/2
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - field_name: /1/2 generation

  - text: |
      From the wire, we know Eve sent `whoami` to Bob's machine. Did her own log keep a trace of it?

      👋 Search for it: `/whoami`.
    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: /1/2
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - caret: '"whoami"'

  - text: |
      There it is — in a field 666 the client's schema has never heard of. Eve logs her own contraband.

      👋 Press `t`, then `o`; in the prefilled override, rename the field: `--field-name contraband`. Press Enter.
    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: whoami
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - field_name: '"whoami" contraband'

  - text: |
      The log now looks fully typed: captures, requests, responses, generations, contraband. Let's save our work.

      👋 Press `s` to save the overrides, then Enter.
    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: whoami
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - file_exists: eve/server.yaml

  - text: |
      All done: a schema reverse-crafted from a truncated, type-less blob, saved in eve/server.yaml.

      👋 Press `:q` to quit.
    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: whoami
